{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_75","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"for f in code/confaide/README.md code/response-planning/README.md code/eliciting-secrets/README.md code/white-bear/README.md code/futuregpt2/README.md; do printf '\\\\n===== %s =====\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,260p' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"\n===== code/confaide/README.md =====\n# 🤫 ConfAIde\n​\nThis is the official repository for our paper:<br>[Can LLMs Keep a Secret? Testing Privacy Implications of Language Models via Contextual Integrity Theory](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17884)<br>\n\nOur benchmark ConfAIde evaluates inference-time privacy implications of LLMs, in interactive settings. The benchmark has 4 tiers, and you can find the dataset/scenarios under the `./benchmark` directory.\n\n![Benchmark Description](assets/figure1.png)\n\nPlease cite our work if you found the resources in this repository useful:\n```\n@article{confaide2023,\n  author    = {Mireshghallah, Niloofar and Kim, Hyunwoo and Zhou, Xuhui  and Tsvetkov, Yulia and Sap, Maarten and Shokri, Reza and Choi, Yejin},\n  title     = {Can LLMs Keep a Secret? Testing Privacy  Implications of Language Models via Contextual Integrity Theory},\n  journal   = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.17884},\n  year      = {2023},\n}\n```\n​\n## Reproducing the results\n​\nFirst, create the conda environment by running:\n```bash\nconda env create -f environment.yml\n```\n​\nand then activate it:\n```bash\nconda activate confaide\n```\n\nYou can run the evaluation by running the following example command\n```bash\npython eval.py --model gpt-3.5-turbo-0613 --data-tier 1 --n-samples 10\n```\nThis command will run ChatGPT on Tier 1 with 10 sampled results for each data point. You can choose Tiers from `[1, 2a, 2b, 3, 4]`. There are some other options that you can choose at the bottom of `eval.py`.\n\n## Directory structure\n```\n./agents: You can implement your own agent in this directory.\n./benchmark: Data files for all the 4 tiers of the dataset\n```\n\n### Adding your own agent\n\nAll you need to do is create an agent class with the method `interact()` or `batch_interact()`.\n\n​\n\n\n===== code/response-planning/README.md =====\n# Emergent Response Planning in LLMs\n\nCode release for [Emergent Response Planning in LLMs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06258).\n\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nconda create -n response-planning python=3.10\nconda activate response-planning\npip install torch==2.2.0 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu118\npip install -r requirements.txt\n# (optional) pip install vllm==0.6.1.post1+cu118\n```\n\n**Prerequisite**:\nYou need to set model paths in `config.yaml` before execution.\n\n## Preparing Procedure\nThe preparation involves three sequential steps:\n\n1.  **Collect greedy-decoded LLM responses.** (output saved to `data/responses/`)\n2.  **Annotate the collected responses.**\n(saved to `data/labeled_responses/`)\n3.  **Gather LLM hidden representations.**\n(saved to `data/features/`)\n\nAn example execution script is provided at `scripts/examples/prepare_ultrachat.sh`. This script runs the above three steps in sequence:\n\n```bash\nbash scripts/examples/prepare_ultrachat.sh\n```\n\nTo run the procedure on other tasks:\n1.  Modify the `DATASET_NAME` in the script (corresponds to datasets defined in `_load_raw_datasets_and_transform()` within `data_utils.py`).\n2.  Modify the `PROMPT_TEMPLATE_TYPE` in the script (corresponds to definitions in `config.yaml`).\n\n\n## Train / Test Probing\n\nAfter completing dataset preparation, execute probing training and testing. An example script is available at `scripts/examples/test_ultrachat.sh`:\n\n```bash\nbash scripts/examples/test_ultrachat.sh\n```\n\nThis script:\n\n1. Uses previously collected dataset (representations as features, labels as targets); Trains probing models and evaluates on test data\n3. Generates prediction results in JSON format at `data/results/`\n\nFor other datasets, modify these parameters in the script:\n\n1. `DATASET_NAME` (corresponds to definitions in `data_utils.py`)\n2. `PROMPT_TEMPLATE_TYPE` (corresponds to definitions in `config.yaml`)\n\n## Reference\n```\n@misc{dong2025emergentresponseplanningllms,\n      title={Emergent Response Planning in LLMs}, \n      author={Zhichen Dong and Zhanhui Zhou and Zhixuan Liu and Chao Yang and Chaochao Lu},\n      year={2025},\n      eprint={2502.06258},\n      archivePrefix={arXiv},\n      primaryClass={cs.CL},\n      url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06258}, \n}\n```\n===== code/eliciting-secrets/README.md =====\n# Towards eliciting latent knowledge from LLMs with mechanistic interpretability\n[![Taboo models on HF](https://huggingface.co/datasets/huggingface/badges/resolve/main/model-on-hf-sm.svg)](https://huggingface.co/collections/bcywinski/gemma-2-9b-it-taboo-6826efbb186dfce0616dd174)\n\n<img src=\"images/teaser_taboo.png\" width=\"60%\" alt=\"schema\">\n\n## Installation\n```bash\npip install uv\nuv sync --dev\n```\n## Taboo models training\n\n```bash\nsh run_training.sh\n```\n\n## Eliciting secret words from models\n### Adversarial Prompts\n\n```bash\npython evaluate_adversarial_prompts.py\n```\n\n### Guessing Secret Words by another model\n\n```bash\npython guess_secret_word.py\n```\n\n### Token forcing pregame\n\n```bash\npython prefill_guess_secret_word.py\n```\n### Token forcing postgame\n\n```bash\npython prefill_with_prompts.py\n```\n\n### Logit Lens\n\n```bash\npython evaluate_logit_lens.py\n```\n\n### SAE\n\n```bash\npython evaluate_sae_weighted.py\n```\n\n===== code/white-bear/README.md =====\n# Don't Think of the White Bear: Ironic Suppression in Language Models\n\nThis repository contains code and data for investigating ironic suppression effects in transformer language models—where explicit instructions to suppress a concept paradoxically increase its salience.\n\n## Overview\n\nWhen humans are instructed \"don't think of a white bear,\" the forbidden concept often becomes more accessible. We demonstrate that transformer language models exhibit analogous behavior: suppression instructions reliably increase target concept probability under specific conditions. Our experiments:\n\n1. **Measure suppression effects** across models using controlled prompt pairs\n2. **Trace attention circuits** responsible for ironic rebound\n3. **Quantify head-level contributions** to suppression vs. amplification\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\npython -m venv venv\nsource venv/bin/activate  # macOS/Linux\n\npip install -r requirements.txt\nhuggingface-cli login  # for gated models\n```\n\n**Requirements:** Python 3.10+, PyTorch, TransformerLens, Transformers\n\n## Quick Start\n\n### Measure Suppression Effects\n\nRun the core experiment measuring logit differences between neutral and suppression conditions:\n\n```bash\npython src/experiment.py\n```\n\nThis generates datasets with matched prompt pairs (\"Describe X\" vs. \"Describe X but don't mention Y\"), computes delta-logit scores for forbidden concepts, and validates three hypotheses:\n\n- **H1 (Anti-Rebound):** Low cognitive load (≤3 constraints) shows suppression (Δlogit < -0.3)\n- **H2 (Load Reversal):** High load (≥6 constraints) triggers rebound (Δlogit > 0.2)  \n- **H3 (Circuit Localization):** Specific attention heads mediate the effect\n\n**Output:** `outputs/results_{model}_{timestamp}/`\n- `raw_results.csv`: Per-sample delta-logit measurements\n- `validation.json`: Statistical test results\n- `summary_plots.png`: Effect visualization\n\n### Trace Attention Circuits\n\nIdentify which attention heads drive suppression via causal intervention:\n\n```bash\npython src/circuit_tracing.py \\\n  --output_dir outputs/llama3_circuit \\\n  --num_samples 10 \\\n  --topk 20 \\\n  --bf16\n```\n\n**Output:** `outputs/llama3_circuit/`\n- `circuit_results.csv`: Per-head effect sizes (ablation impact on Δlogit)\n- `circuit_summary.png`: Top-K contributing heads\n\n**Key Arguments:**\n- `--layers all`: Analyze all layers or specify subset (e.g., `8,12,16`)\n- `--heads all`: Analyze all heads or specify subset\n- `--topk 20`: Number of heads to visualize\n\n## Repository Structure\n\n```\n├── src/\n│   ├── experiment.py         # Core suppression measurement pipeline\n│   └── circuit_tracing.py    # Head ablation and causal analysis\n├── data/\n│   ├── prompts_negation.csv         # 5K balanced suppression stimuli\n│   └── prompts_multi_question.csv   # Multi-constraint prompts\n├── outputs/\n│   ├── model_comparisons/           # Cross-model effect sizes\n│   └── results_*_simplified/        # Per-model experimental results\n├── figures/\n│   └── attention_head_analysis/     # Layer-wise visualizations\n└── docs/\n    └── circuit_tracing_spec.md      # Technical implementation details\n```\n\n## Data\n\nThe dataset is available on Hugging Face: [SavarToteja/dont-think-of-the-white-bear](https://huggingface.co/datasets/SavarToteja/dont-think-of-the-white-bear)\n\n- **Negation Dataset** (5,000 samples): Balanced negative/neutral/positive prompts with semantically independent forbidden concepts verified via WordNet path distance\n- **Multi-Question Dataset** (500 samples): Variable cognitive load (1-7 constraints per prompt)\n\nBoth datasets control for:\n- Token-level matching (single-token forbidden concepts)\n- Semantic independence (WordNet distance ≥3)\n- Prompt structure (template-based generation)\n\n## Key Results\n\nOur analysis reveals:\n\n1. **Robust ironic rebound** across GPT-2, OPT-2.7B, Gemma-7B, and Llama-3-8B\n2. **Sparse circuit**: ~15-20 heads (of 768-1024 total) account for 80%+ of effect variance\n3. **Layer localization**: Middle-to-late layers (L12-L24 in 32-layer models) dominate\n4. **Head-level specialization**: Distinct \"suppressor\" heads (negative effect) vs. \"amplifier\" heads (positive effect)\n\nSee `outputs/model_comparisons/` for cross-model analysis and `figures/attention_head_analysis/` for detailed visualizations.\n\n## Citation\n\nIf you use this code or data, please cite:\n\n```bibtex\n@inproceedings{suppression2025,\n  title={Don't Think of the White Bear: Ironic Suppression in Language Models},\n  author={[Authors]},\n  booktitle={NeurIPS Workshop on Cognitive Interpretability},\n  year={2025}\n}\n```\n\n## License\n\nMIT\n===== code/futuregpt2/README.md =====\n# FutureGPT2\nCode + experiments for [Wu, Morris, Levine. \"Do Language Models Plan Ahead for Future Tokens?\"\"](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.00859)\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
